From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 12:56:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] inkobj: Save and load Ink Serialized Format.

Office asks whether the ink platform is there, and when it is, it loads an
ISF stream into a new InkDisp before it creates any other ink object; a
failing Load leaves it with no ink objects at all, so the slide show pen
draws nothing.  With the platform absent it takes a path where new ink
keeps no InkML, and a kept slide show annotation saves as an empty
ppt/ink/ink1.xml.

Load takes raw ISF, "base64:" text and the comment extension of a GIF;
Save writes raw ISF and base64 with each compression mode (GIF output is
not done).  The decoder handles the GUID table, the ink space rectangle,
drawing attributes (the mantissa of pen sizes, custom attributes), stroke
descriptors with buttons and stroke property lists, metrics, the float and
double transform tables, packet data compressed with the default Huffman
tables or bit packing with and without delta-delta, property data
compressed by bit packing or LZ with embedded variant types, point
property blocks and unknown tags with sizes.  The format follows
Microsoft's published ISF specification; tag values, the default Huffman
tables and the drawing flags follow WPF's MIT-licensed implementation.

Not measured on Windows yet (tools/isfprobe): loading into ink that has
strokes (refused, as WPF does), the dirty flag after Load, the error codes
for bad arguments, and the "base64:" header on Save.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

